1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,800
Wednesday, three days before Malika's wedding site opens.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:11,136
Dilnoza has one step left on its card, and it is the one the processing centre cares about.

3
00:00:11,186 --> 00:00:26,064
In Lesson 21, the wedding site was switched on for one card, and in Lesson 8, Timur's card zone spoke to each processing centre through its own adapter, and this lesson gives each website and card the terminal those centres know it by.

4
00:00:26,114 --> 00:00:29,256
In a shop, you pay at a card machine on the counter.

5
00:00:29,306 --> 00:00:36,456
Online there is no counter, but the processing centre still wants to know which machine sent each charge.

6
00:00:36,506 --> 00:00:40,800
It accepts charges only from machines it has issued.

7
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:48,048
Send a charge with the wrong numbers, and the money is refused, or worse, booked to another merchant.

8
00:00:48,098 --> 00:00:53,904
So each online payment goes through a virtual card machine: an e-POS terminal.

9
00:00:53,954 --> 00:01:01,248
Its identity is a pair of numbers that the processing centre issues: a merchant number and a terminal number.

10
00:01:01,298 --> 00:01:04,944
On screen they are called the MID and the TID.

11
00:01:04,994 --> 00:01:07,968
Dilnoza opens the website's page.

12
00:01:08,018 --> 00:01:17,928
There is a section called e-POS terminals, with a hint: each card needs one live terminal from the processing centre, or its payments are refused.

13
00:01:17,978 --> 00:01:24,552
On a website with no cards chosen yet, the "Register e-POS terminal" button is grey.

14
00:01:24,602 --> 00:01:29,160
Malika's site already takes one card, so the button is live.

15
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:31,032
Dilnoza clicks it.

16
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:35,568
A form asks for the MID, the TID and the card.

17
00:01:35,618 --> 00:01:39,504
She types the pair the centre issued, and chooses the card.

18
00:01:39,554 --> 00:01:44,976
A bank admin registers each pair for one website and one card.

19
00:01:45,026 --> 00:01:51,888
There is only one live terminal per website and card, so each payment has exactly one way out.

20
00:01:51,938 --> 00:01:59,544
Where the processing centre requires it, the terminal is registered with the centre too, through Timur's card zone.

21
00:01:59,594 --> 00:02:02,280
It is kept only if the centre accepts it.

22
00:02:02,330 --> 00:02:07,584
If the centre refuses, or does not answer, nothing is saved.

23
00:02:07,634 --> 00:02:09,456
Here it accepts.

24
00:02:09,506 --> 00:02:12,552
The page says the terminal is registered.

25
00:02:12,602 --> 00:02:16,752
It appears in the list as Live, with who registered it.

26
00:02:16,802 --> 00:02:21,936
Next to it are three buttons: Suspend, Resume and Revoke.

27
00:02:21,986 --> 00:02:26,736
Suspend stops its payments on the bank's side alone, and it can be resumed.

28
00:02:26,786 --> 00:02:28,752
Revoking is final.

29
00:02:28,802 --> 00:02:31,872
Dilnoza opens Revoke, just to read it.

30
00:02:31,922 --> 00:02:38,472
The window warns that a revoked pair never comes back, and asks why, for the record.

31
00:02:38,522 --> 00:02:41,016
She closes it without revoking.

32
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:44,928
Every change is written down: who, what, and why.

33
00:02:44,978 --> 00:02:50,600
So can the pair of a closed terminal ever be used again?

34
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:56,192
A pair is never registered twice, not even after its terminal is closed.

35
00:02:56,242 --> 00:03:01,928
Think of a licence plate: issued once, one per car, never reused.

36
00:03:01,978 --> 00:03:07,160
Teams are tempted to reuse an old pair to save a call to the centre.

37
00:03:07,210 --> 00:03:15,728
I think that is the most expensive shortcut in this module, because a pair used twice mixes two merchants' histories forever.

38
00:03:15,778 --> 00:03:19,424
Now the card at the top, "Before it takes payments".

39
00:03:19,474 --> 00:03:24,896
The terminal step is ticked: every card has a live e-POS terminal.

40
00:03:24,946 --> 00:03:28,928
With the fee from the last lesson, every step is done.

41
00:03:28,978 --> 00:03:33,632
The card turns green and says "Ready: taking payments".

42
00:03:33,682 --> 00:03:40,088
And before every charge, the payment asks for the live terminal of its website and card.

43
00:03:40,138 --> 00:03:44,240
No terminal, no charge, and no money moves.

44
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:50,912
On Saturday, the first wedding order arrives, and it goes out through exactly one machine.

45
00:03:50,962 --> 00:04:00,272
To recap: each website and card has exactly one live e-POS terminal, and its pair is never reused.

46
00:04:00,322 --> 00:04:05,584
So what happens to a charge with no live terminal?

47
00:04:05,634 --> 00:04:08,704
It is refused, and no money moves.

48
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:13,576
Next: how the bank knows a request really comes from the merchant.
